    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's what I implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change rejects `POST`/`PUT` owner requests with **400** when the email domain is on a disposable-domain blocklist (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`).\n\n**Where the logic lives \u2014 kept each piece in its natural home:**\n\n1. **`OwnerEmail`** (the shared email helper that already owns validity + normalization) \u2014 added a small `isDisposable(String email)` method plus the `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` set. Domain-parsing knowledge stays with the other email logic; the blocklist is defined once and reused by both pipelines.\n\n2. **`ValidateOwnerEmailDomain`** (new function class) \u2014 a dedicated single-responsibility step mirroring the existing `ValidateOwnerPostcode`/`ValidateOwnerRegistrationDate` pattern. It reads the already-normalized body variable, skips an absent email, and throws the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of(\"email\"))` (already handled as 400) when the domain is disposable. I deliberately did **not** overload `NormalizeOwnerEmail`, whose job is normalization \u2014 blocklist rejection is a separate concern.\n\n3. **Pipeline wiring** \u2014 inserted `validateEmailDomain` right after `normalizeEmail` in both `owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml`, so the check runs on the lower-cased email before any validation or persistence (and under the PUT transaction governance).\n\n**Reuse:** no new exception, handler, or DTO \u2014 the fix rides on the existing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` \u2192 400 machinery and the existing `OwnerEmail` helper.\n\nTests: the `AcceptanceTest.errorRejectsDisposableDomain` case passes and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
